Actually, MOFs as luminescent sensors have been widely studied.10-13 Especially, lanthanide-based MOFs (Ln-MOFs) have attracted enormous attention due to unique optical properties, such as the large Stokes shifts, unexceptionable quantum yields, high purity of colors, and long lifetime on the emission through an antenna effect.14,15 Up to now, a lot of Ln-MOFs luminescent sensors have been applied to detecting anions,16,17 cations,18-20 organic small molecules,21-24 and so on. Besides, these luminescent sensors display excellent chemical stabilities, high sensitivity, and good reusability.25 Inspired by these cases, it is advisable to design and construct Ln-MOFs as highly selective, sensitive and recyclable sensors for detecting quercetin.
In this contribution, a novel metal organic framework $\{\mathrm{[Tb_{3}(C B A)_{2}(H C O O)(\mu_{3}{-}O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)]{\cdot}2H_{2}O{\cdot}0.5D M F}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (S-1) was constructed from 1,1-cyclobutanedicarboxylic acid ligand $\mathrm{(H_{2}C B A)}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ by solvothermal reaction at 130 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which displays a 3D honeycomb array with cubane clusterbased chains. S-1 has good thermal stability and solvent/pH stability. Especially for water stability, S-1 still retains intact structure after exposure to water for 10 weeks or boiling water for 10 weeks. More importantly, S-1 is the first example of MOFs as luminescent sensor for efficiently detecting quercetin with recyclable performance.
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis confirmed that S-1 crystallizes in the trigonal system with space group $R{-}3$ . The asymmetric unit contains three independent Tb ions, two $\mathrm{CBA}^{2-}$ ligands, one HCOO- anion, four $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups and one $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure 1a). Two Tb1, one Tb2 and one Tb3 ions are bridged by four $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups to form a $\left[\mathrm{Tb}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right]$ cubane cluster, which is further extended to an infinite chain with a zigzag type by sharing the vertex Tb1 along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ axis (Figure 1b). The distance of adjacent cubane centres is $4.60\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and the angles formed from three adjacent cubane centres are all $121.10^{\circ}$ (Figure S2). The chain is stabilized by HCOO- anion (coordination mode: $\mu_{4}–\kappa\mathrm{O}5$ : $\kappa{\bf O}5$ : $\kappa{\cal O}6$ : $\kappa{\cal O}6$ ) (Figure S3c) coming from the in situ decomposition of DMF.26 Then, six chains are interconnected by $\mathrm{CBA}^{2-}$ ligand with two coordination modes $\overbrace{\mu_{3}–\kappa}\mathrm{O}7$ : κO8: $\kappa{\mathrm O}9$ , O10 and $\mu_{3}–\kappa\mathrm{O}11$ : $\kappa\mathrm{O}12$ , O13: $\kappa\mathrm{O}14$ ) (Figure $\mathrm{S3a-b}$ ), which result in a 1D hexagonal channel with the diameter of $5.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure 1c). As shown in Figure 1d, each 1D unit is further linked to six neighboring and equivalent structures, forming 3D ordered honeycomb array. In the previous investigations, cubane as clusters are mostly linked by ligand to form framework, such as $[\mathrm{Cu_{4}I_{4}(D A B C O)_{2}}].$ ,27 and $\{\mathrm{[Gd_{4}M n(L)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{3}(}\mu_{3}-}$ $\mathrm{OH)_{4}(H C O O)_{1.5}]\cdot(N O_{3})_{2.5}\cdot6H_{2}O_{\mathrm{\Delta}},^{26}}$ etc, while the example of cubane clusters extending into chain by sharing the cubane is rare in MOFs, and only three such materials like $\mathrm{[Er_{6}(O H)_{8}(p y d c)_{5}(H_{2}O)_{3}]_{\Omega}}^{2}$ 8 $[\mathrm{Li}_{4}(\mathrm{PyEP})_{3}(\mathrm{OH})]^{29}$ and $[\mathrm{Gd_{3}C u_{12}I_{12}(I N)_{9}(D M F)_{4}]_{n}\cdot n D M F^{30}}$ were reported. Hence, S-1 is a rather rare example of honeycomb-like metal organic framework constructed by cubane cluster-based chains.
The PXRD pattern of S-1 was recorded in Figure S4. The peaks of as-synthesized sample are in accordance with the simulated ones, showing that S-1 is pure phase. To investigate the chemical stability of S-1, the as-synthesized sample was immersed in seven common solvents (methanol, ethanol, acetonitrile, dichloromethane, DMA, DMF and water) for about $10~\mathrm{{h}}.$ , respectively. The PXRD analysis demonstrated that S-1 was stable in these solvents beacuse of the high consistence between the measured and simulated peaks (Figure S5a). Considering that there may be exist excellent water stability in S-1 due to strong Tb–O bonds in $\left[\mathrm{Tb}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right]$ cubane cluster,31 S-1 was soaked in water for 10 weeks or boiling water for 10 weeks. The PXRD results proved that S-1 still kept crystallinity, verifying high water stability (Figure S6). Besides, when the sample was immersed in a series of aqueous solutions with different $\mathfrak{p H}$ values from 1 to 14 for about $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively, the framework of S-1 is almost intact in $\mathsf{p H}$ values from 1 to 13, which can be proved by PXRD analysis (Figure S5b). The thermal stability of S-1 was studied by TGA in ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere (Figure S7). A weight loss of $9.35\%$ could be seen from 70 to $310~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is attributed to the loss of free and coordinated $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and free DMF molecules (Calcd: $9.37\%$ ). Finally, S-1 became amorphous state gradually over $310~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Therefore, S-1 has good chemical stability and high thermal stability.
The solid-state luminescence spectrum of S-1 was displayed in Figure S8. Under excitation at $280~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , S-1 appears four characteristic emission peaks at 489, 544, 585, and $621~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which belong to $\mathrm{{}^{5}D_{4}\mathrm{{\to}^{7}F_{\mathrm{{J}}}}}$ $(\mathrm{J}=6,5,4,3)$ transitions of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions,32 respectively. Besides, the $^5{\mathrm{D}}_{4}\rightarrow^{7}{\mathrm{F}}_{5}$ transition at $544~\mathrm{nm}$ is strongest, showing green-light emission, which can be seen by the naked eye under UV light with $254~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S8, inset). The quantum yield is $53.06\%$ (Figure S9) and the lifetime of solid S-1 sample is $0.650~\mathrm{ms}$ at the strongest emission of $544~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S10). Moreover, the emission spectra of S-1 before and after stored in water for two days were measured (Figure S11). Luminescent intensity showed almost no change, revealing excellent luminescent property and water stability of S-1, which motivates us to study it acting as a luminescent sensor in the aqueous solution.
In order to explore the influence of quercetin on the luminescence of S-1, the ground sample was dispersed in aqueous solution $(6~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}})_{,}$ ) to generate a suspension by ultrasound. As shown in Figure 2, when a methanol solution of quercetin was dropped into suspension of S-1 with gradually increasing concentration, the luminescent intensity obviously decreased. The color which can be observed by the naked eye under UV light $(254~\mathrm{nm})$ obviously changed from green to colorless (inset of Figure 2). Therefore, quercetin exhibits a quenching effect on the luminescence of S-1. To further study the quenching effect of quercetin, the luminescence intensity ratio vs. quercetin concentration plot was provided (Figure 3). In the concentration range of $0-300\ \mathrm{ppm}$ (namely, $0-993~{\upmu\mathrm{M}})$ , it linearly obeys $\mathrm{I}_{0}/\mathrm{I}$ $=1.143+0.129$ [quercetin], which is close to the Stern – Volmer equation: $\mathrm{I_{0}/I~=~1~+~K_{s v}[}$ [quercetin]33 (Here, $\mathrm{I}_{0}$ and I are the luminescence intensity of S-1 before and after the addition of quercetin, respectively; [quercetin] is the concentration of quercetin, and $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{sv}}$ is the quenching rate constant). Lowering the concentration of S-1 suspension $(3~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}),$ ), the relationship of luminescence intensity ratio vs. quercetin concentration is still linear (Figures S12 and 13). ${\sf K}_{\mathrm{sv}}$ of $1.29\times10^{5}\mathrm{~mL~g^{-1}}$ (namely, $3.89~\times~10^{4}~\mathrm{{\textperthousand}}$ for quercetin was got with the correlation coefficient of 0.998, which indicated a high quenching efficiency on the emission of S-1 $(6\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})_{,}$ ). The LOD was calculated to be $0.23\mathrm{ppm}$ ${7.6~\times~10^{-7}~\mathrm{{M})}}$ based on $3\sigma/k$ ( $\scriptstyle{\overbar{\boldsymbol{\sigma}}}$ represents the relative standard deviation (RSD) of the blank measurement for
10 times; $k$ represents the slope from linear fit of the relative luminescence intensity versus concentration in the linear range).34- 36 Furthermore, the LOD for quercetin using other detection methods were listed in Table S2, and the LOD falls into a range of $0.5\ \mathrm{\nM}\ -\ 14\ \mathrm{\upmuM}.$ . Hence, S-1 can act as a high sensitive luminescent sensor and be comparable to these methods.
In practical applications, not only should the sensor have high sensitivity but also high selectivity to the sensing objectives. Therefore, the selectivity test of S-1 toward some potentially coexisting interferences, such as cations, anions and small molecules, was carried out. Herein, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{(1~mg~mL^{-1})}$ ) of $\mathrm{{Na}}_{\mathrm{{n}}}X$ solution $\mathrm{(X=HPO}_{4}\mathrm{{^{2-}}}$ , $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ -, Cl-, respectively), $\mathrm{YCl_{m}}$ solution (Y ${\bf\Lambda}={\bf K}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ , respectively), or small organic molecule solution (urea, glutamic acid, soluble starch, and glucose) was added into the above mentioned S-1 suspension $(50~\mathrm{\ppm})$ , respectively. As shown in Figure S14, the luminescence intensity almost had no influence on the emission of S-1 with addition of interferent. Meanwhile, the interference of methanol was also conduced because quercetin was dissolved by methanol. When $100~\mathrm{\textmuL}$ of methanol was dropped into S-1 suspension, luminescence intensity was nearly the same as the original one. Besides, when $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ interferent and $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ quercetin ( $\mathrm{1\mg\mL^{-}}$ 1) were added together, quenching effect occurred and the degree of quenching had negligible effect. On the other hand, there are some other flavone analogues and polyphenols like apigenin, isorhamnetin, hesperidin, catechin, catechol, resorcin and hydroquinone in practical detection, and their chemical structures were shown in Figure S15. Here, we investigated the selectivity of S-1 to detect quercetin. As illustrated in Figure 4, only quercetin can effectively quench the luminescence of S-1, but the others do not. Thus, S-1 exhibits high selectivity to detect quercetin and is expected to be used in practice.
As for recyclability of quercetin sensor, it is seldom mentinoned in the reported literatures.9 However, it is important to reuse sensor by a simple and fast method. Hence, in order to study the recyclable performance of S-1, the used sample was washed with ice acetic/methanol (1:10, v:v) for several times, and then the crystal recovered original white color (Figure S16, inset). The PXRD also indicated the framework of S-1 still remains intact (Figure S15). Meanwhile, luminescent intensity showed almost no change with $99\%$ quenching efficiency after six runs (Figure 5). In addition, $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ leaching was detected by ICP, and no $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion remained in the filtrate. Therefore, S-1 is extremely stable and can be reused at least six times without loss in luminescence response by fast and convenient way.
The developed S-1 sensor was applied to detect quercetin in the onion sample to evaluate the practicality. $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ onion extract was added to the suspension of S-1, and then the luminescence intensity was recorded. The content of quecretin in the onion skin and apple peel sample was 24.5 and $8.5~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , respectively, calculated by the concentration linear range, which was well consistent with the result of 25.2 and $8.7~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ determined by HPLC-MS (Figure S17). Therefore, the proposed method is accurate and reliable.
Furthermore, in order to make S-1 sensor more portable, the test paper was prepared by immersing filter paper $(2.0\times0.5~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ on the S-1 suspension and drying in air. Then, the test paper was soaked in a series of different concentration of quercetin for several seconds and dried at room temperature. The luminescence changes of these dried test papers were recorded under the irradiation of UV light $(254~\mathrm{nm})$ ). As shown in Figure 6, the green color of the test paper became weaker and weaker, and finally colorless as the concentration of quercetin increased from 0 to 1 mg $\mathrm{mL^{-1}}$ . Hence, the identifiable colors exhibit the value of practical application.
The possible quenching mechanism is further discussed. Firstly, quercetin was added into S-1 suspension, forming a yellow solution. After centrifugation, the color of solid sample turned yellow and the color of solution became shallow (Figure S18, inset), indicating that S-1 can effectively adsorb quercetin. Furthermore, the luminescence lifetimes of S-1 with and without quercetin were explored (Figure S19), and the large difference between $0.607~\mathrm{~ms~}$ and 0.595 ms indicated that significant interaction exists between S-1 and quercetin. Because the pore size of S-1 is smaller than that of quercetin (Figure S20), this adsorption maybe benefits from the hydrogen bonding interaction between S-1 surface and quercetin molecules, which is consistent with the conclusion of single crystal quenching experiment (Figure S21). Ultraviolet-Visible (UV-Vis) absorption was measured to explain the adsorption behavior of S-1 toward quercetin (Figure S18). As the time increased, the absorbance gradually reduced, implying the decrease of quercetin content. Therefore, S-1 can effectively accumulate quercetin, and thus improve the selectivity and sensitivity of detection. Meanwhile, the adsorption of quercetin in S-1 can result in a tremendous change for the luminescence of S-1.9,37 Secondly, the PXRD results indicated that the framework of S-1 keeps intact after six cycles, revealing that luminescent quenching did not originate from the collapse of framework. In order to further explore the energy transfer, the singlet-state and the triplet excited-state energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{CBA}$ was calculated to be $36364~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and 24691 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ based on the UV–Vis absorption spectra of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}C B A}$ (Figure S22) and the phosphorescence of isostructural Gd-MOF at $77~\mathrm{K}$ (Figure S23), respectively. The energy gap is $11673~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , larger than $5000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which indicates that the intersystem crossing process (ISC) is efficient.38 The energy level of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ( ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}$ , 20500 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) well matches with triplet excited-state energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{CBA}$ , which can efficiently sensitize the emission of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ion.39 Nevertheless, the excited-state energy level of quercetin is 23148 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ based on the UV–Vis absorption spectra of quercetin (Figure S20), locating between the energy level of ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}$ from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and triplet excited-state energy of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}C B A}$ (Figure 7). After quercetin is introduced into the system, quercetin can obtain the energy from $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}C B A}$ by ISC methods, suppressing the energy transfer from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{CBA}$ to $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , thus resulting in the luminescent quenching.34,38 Furthermore, the UV-Vis absorption bands of quercetin and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{CBA}$ partly overlap for each other (Figure S22), and the competition for the irradiated light absorption between the quercetin and the ligand can also cause the luminescence quenching.38 Finally, the Stern−Volmer equation was linear at the testing concentrations, proving that the existence of dynamic quenching based on collision between S-1 and quercetin in this process.40
In conclusion, a 3D honeycomb arrayed metal-organic framework S-1 with cubane cluster-based chains was successfully obtained, which exhibits excellent water stability and still retains intact structure after immersing in water for 10 weeks or boiling water for 10 weeks. S-1 is the first example of MOFs as luminescent sensor for efficiently detecting quercetin and LOD is as low as $0.23\ \mathrm{ppm}$ $(0.76~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ . The concentration linear range is from 0 to $300\ \mathrm{\ppm}$ $(0\mathrm{~-~}993\mathrm{~}\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , which allows quantitative detection of quercetin. Importantly, S-1 can be reused at least six times with almost no the decrease of luminescent intensity. Furthermore, the proposed method was successfully used to determine the trace quercetin in the real onion skin and apple peel sample. More interestingly, S-1 test paper is also developed to make it more portable and exhibits the value of practical application.
